1
00:00:00,220 --> 00:00:08,560
Director General, um, imagine the sheer burden on your systems when a learner's data has to be manually rekeyed across, you know, five different government departments.

2
00:00:09,180 --> 00:00:09,940
Right. It's, uh,

3
00:00:10,600 --> 00:00:11,640
it's incredibly inefficient.

4
00:00:12,060 --> 00:00:15,400
Yeah. And for your ministry, it's really a symptom of a much deeper issue.

5
00:00:15,940 --> 00:00:18,040
Your internal data just isn't joined up.

6
00:00:18,360 --> 00:00:26,080
So today we're doing a deep dive into how we move from that fragmented government data to a, uh, a single authoritative digital register for learners.

7
00:00:26,760 --> 00:00:27,240
Exactly.

8
00:00:27,980 --> 00:00:32,720
I mean, if you look at the reality of learner data right now, it is heavily siloed.

9
00:00:33,400 --> 00:00:38,360
But the GovStack digital registry specification, uh, version three point o dash alpha,

10
00:00:38,960 --> 00:00:43,760
it actually gives us a clear architectural solution to fix that fragmentation.

11
00:00:44,540 --> 00:00:46,700
So let's look at that fragmentation first.

12
00:00:46,860 --> 00:00:50,380
Um, think about the different lists of learners your country has right now.

13
00:00:50,820 --> 00:00:55,860
The schools keep one, the examination body keeps another, and, uh, a donor project probably keeps a third.

14
00:00:56,560 --> 00:00:59,660
Yeah, and that happens for very pragmatic reasons usually.

15
00:00:59,980 --> 00:01:08,160
You know, one donor is funding a school census, another is, uh, doing a cash transfer for girls, and a third is updating the exam system.

16
00:01:08,700 --> 00:01:13,100
And each of them just builds their own standalone list because they're on a strict timeline.

17
00:01:13,860 --> 00:01:18,140
So if every donor project builds its own siloed list just to deliver on time,

18
00:01:18,920 --> 00:01:21,800
what is the actual consequence for the ministry's ability to operate?

19
00:01:22,160 --> 00:01:24,920
Well, the consequence is massively duplicated data.

20
00:01:25,180 --> 00:01:27,320
I mean, nobody knows which list is actually right.

21
00:01:27,800 --> 00:01:33,060
You can't definitively answer basic questions like, "How many learners do you actually have in the system?"

22
00:01:33,780 --> 00:01:35,640
Wow, so you're just-- you're flying blind.

23
00:01:36,500 --> 00:01:40,680
Yeah. You're flying blind when you try to allocate budgets or, you know, plan infrastructure.

24
00:01:41,260 --> 00:01:44,280
So to fix this, we look at the GovStack specification, right?

25
00:01:44,740 --> 00:01:48,780
It says we need to stop treating these lists as static spreadsheets and, uh,

26
00:01:49,420 --> 00:01:51,200
build them as authoritative services.

27
00:01:51,820 --> 00:01:54,640
Right. A register is an authoritative service.

28
00:01:54,840 --> 00:02:04,180
It's not just a shared drive. It needs a schema which, uh, validates the data format before it enters, and an interface so other systems can securely request data.

29
00:02:04,860 --> 00:02:07,580
And it needs strict access roles and a change log, obviously.

30
00:02:08,080 --> 00:02:14,060
But the reference architecture breaks this into two specific building blocks, um, registration and the digital registry.

31
00:02:14,220 --> 00:02:15,240
How do those work together?

32
00:02:15,880 --> 00:02:21,680
So registration is the intake process. It's where a person applies and an officer actually decides to approve them.

33
00:02:22,400 --> 00:02:27,720
The digital registry is the vault. It keeps that approved record and, you know, serves it to others.

34
00:02:28,220 --> 00:02:32,380
Procurement rules might make, you know, individual siloed contracts cheaper in the short term, though.

35
00:02:32,500 --> 00:02:39,420
So isn't whole of government planning the only way to actually make this architecture reusable across state registries?

36
00:02:39,920 --> 00:02:45,780
Absolutely. I mean, if you don't use whole of government planning, you end up paying to build that same vault fifty times.

37
00:02:46,360 --> 00:02:50,280
If you do it holistically, you build it once, and every sector calls it.

38
00:02:51,020 --> 00:02:55,100
Let's ground this with Progessa. It's, uh, it's a fictional country we use as a model.

39
00:02:55,620 --> 00:03:03,380
They actually built the digital pipes connecting their learner registry to a data exchange layer, but it's still not authoritative.

40
00:03:03,980 --> 00:03:06,420
Right, because Progessa missed the fundamentals.

41
00:03:06,980 --> 00:03:15,280
Their registry has no legal basis in the Education Act, no link to a national identity, and, uh, absolutely zero data quality rules.

42
00:03:15,800 --> 00:03:19,380
It sounds like Progessa built the digital pipes but ignored the data quality.

43
00:03:20,060 --> 00:03:26,540
How does a disciplined load actually ensure those, uh, inconsistent school files become a trusted record?

44
00:03:27,160 --> 00:03:30,820
Well, a disciplined load means that a registry is only as good as its data.

45
00:03:31,260 --> 00:03:34,600
When schools send in files, they don't just instantly overwrite the database.

46
00:03:35,100 --> 00:03:36,420
Right. They go to a staging area.

47
00:03:36,760 --> 00:03:39,020
Exactly. They sit in quarantine.

48
00:03:39,900 --> 00:03:44,620
An officer or an automated system applies master data management rules to catch errors.

49
00:03:45,280 --> 00:03:48,120
Only when a record is approved is it permanently written,

50
00:03:48,740 --> 00:03:52,940
which is, uh, very similar to how UNICEF Giga ensures a single source of truth.

51
00:03:53,820 --> 00:04:02,540
So synthesizing all of this, a register gives every service one authoritative record held as a service and filled through a disciplined load.

52
00:04:02,840 --> 00:04:03,400
Precisely.

53
00:04:04,280 --> 00:04:06,040
Director General, consider this final point.

